+---
+name: document-extraction
+description: "Get text and tables out of PDFs, scans and office documents — pick the extractor by what the file actually is, and verify the output against the page."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [pdf, ocr, pymupdf, marker, tesseract, tables, forms]
+---
+
+# Getting text out of a document
+
+The first decision is not which tool, it is **what kind of document this is**. Text-layer PDF, scan,
+mixed, and forms each fail differently, and using the wrong extractor produces plausible prose with the
+numbers missing — which is the worst outcome, because it reads as success.
+
+## Step 1 — is there a text layer?
+
+```bash
+python3 - <<'PY'
+import fitz                      # pymupdf
+d = fitz.open("input.pdf")
+print("pages:", d.page_count)
+for i in range(min(3, d.page_count)):
+    t = d[i].get_text()
+    print(i, "chars:", len(t), "|", repr(t[:120]))
+PY
+```
+
+- **Hundreds of characters per page** → real text layer. Use it; do not OCR.
+- **Single digits or empty** → a scan. Go to OCR.
+- **A mix** → extract the text pages, OCR the rest, and note which pages came from where.
+
+`pdftotext -layout` is the quickest first look and preserves column position, which matters for tables:
+
+```bash
+pdftotext -layout input.pdf - | head -60
+```
+
+If `-layout` output has clean columns, you have a born-digital document and the job is nearly done.
+
+## Step 2 — pick the extractor
+
+| Document | Use | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| Text-layer PDF | `pymupdf` (`fitz`) | fast, exact, preserves order |
+| Scan, clean | `tesseract` at 300 dpi | cheapest accurate OCR |
+| Scan, complex layout / tables / multi-column | `marker-pdf` | reconstructs structure, slower, needs disk |
+| Office docs | the format's own reader (`python-docx`, `openpyxl`) | never OCR a .docx |
+| Images of text | `tesseract <img> stdout` | and crop to the text first |
+
+```bash
+# OCR one page at 300 dpi (below ~200 dpi accuracy collapses)
+pdftoppm -r 300 -f 1 -l 1 -png input.pdf /tmp/pg && tesseract /tmp/pg-01.png stdout
+
+# whole scanned document with layout reconstruction
+marker_single input.pdf --output_dir /tmp/out
+```
+
+**Crop before you OCR.** A 1920×1080 render costs ~2000 vision/text tokens; a 256×256 crop of the region
+that matters costs ~90. On dense documents this is the difference between one call and twenty.
+
+## Tables
+
+Tables are where extraction silently loses the answer. A table flattened to prose loses the column
+alignment that gave each number its meaning.
+
+- `pdftotext -layout` first: if the columns line up, split on runs of two or more spaces and you have rows.
+- Otherwise `pymupdf`'s `page.find_tables()` → `.to_pandas()`, or `camelot` for ruled tables.
+- **Always print the row and column count and the first two rows** before using the data. A table that
+  came back 12 rows when the page shows 40 is a parse failure, not a short table.
+- A `0` or blank in a parsed numeric field is a parse bug until proven otherwise — check the source's own
+  formatting (thousands separators, parentheses for negatives, unicode minus `−`, a trailing asterisk).
+
+## Step 3 — verify against the page
+
+Extraction is a claim. Check it:
+
+1. **Page count and character count** — print both. A 40-page PDF yielding 200 characters is a failure you
+   can see in one line.
+2. **Spot-check the first and last page** against a render of the same page.
+3. **Grep for a string you know is in the document** — a title, a section number, a proper noun. If it is
+   missing, the extraction is partial.
+4. **If a number is going into a decision**, read it out of an image render of that page as well. Two
+   independent reads of one number is a cheap and high-value habit.
+
+## PDF forms
+
+AcroForm fields are read and written with `pypdf`:
+
+```python
+from pypdf import PdfReader, PdfWriter
+r = PdfReader("form.pdf")
+print(r.get_fields())                      # name -> field object (type, value, rect)
+w = PdfWriter(clone_from=r)
+w.update_page_form_field_values(w.pages[0], {"field_name": "value"})
+w.write("filled.pdf")
+```
+
+- **Print every field name and its type first.** Names are opaque (`topmostSubform[0].Page1[0].f1_02[0]`),
+  and guessing produces a file that looks filled and is not.
+- **Checkboxes are `/Btn` fields** and take either a state name from `field.button_states` (preferred) or a
+  boolean, depending on the producer.
+- **Regenerating a PDF is a rewrite, not a patch.** If you rebuild a form from scratch, every previously
+  filled field must be carried across — including the date next to the signature and any image. Extract
+  all fields from the original first, then diff the new file's fields against that list before delivering.
+- **`pypdf` cannot create a form from nothing.** For an empty form, place text with `reportlab` at measured
+  coordinates, then flatten — and check the result by extracting the text back out.
+- **Flatten means frozen.** Do not flatten a form someone may need to edit later; ask, or deliver both.
+
+## Arxiv and web PDFs
+
+```bash
+curl -sL "https://arxiv.org/abs/<id>"       # abstract page, cheap
+curl -sL "https://arxiv.org/pdf/<id>" -o /tmp/p.pdf && pdftotext -layout /tmp/p.pdf -
+```
+
+Fetch the PDF once, extract locally, and read from the file — repeatedly re-fetching a large PDF to grep a
+different section is the slow path.
+
+## Pitfalls
+
+- **OCR at low dpi.** Under ~200 dpi, small type and thin strokes vanish; 300 dpi is the floor for text.
+- **Rotated pages.** A page scanned sideways returns nothing useful. Check `page.rotation` and fix it
+  before OCR.
+- **Multi-column layouts** intersperse columns line by line when read naively. Use an extractor that
+  respects reading order, or crop column by column.
+- **Headers and footers contaminate every page.** Strip the top and bottom band when you are searching for
+  a value across pages — otherwise the footer's page number is what you find.
+- **A document that is an image inside a PDF wrapper** (a scanned page saved as PDF) has a text layer of
+  zero characters. That is the scan case, and no amount of "just extract the text" will work.
