+---
+name: image-gen-local
+description: "Generate images on a local ComfyUI: drive it by API, find the parameters a workflow exposes, and know when the machine cannot do the job."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [comfyui, diffusion, image, workflow, gpu, vram]
+---
+
+# Image generation on a local ComfyUI
+
+ComfyUI is two layers and they are worth keeping separate:
+
+- **The server** — a graph executor listening on `127.0.0.1:8188` with an HTTP API and a WebSocket.
+- **A workflow** — a JSON graph describing nodes and links. The **API format** is the one that matters:
+  it is a flat map of node id → `class_type` + `inputs`, and it is what you POST.
+
+The UI format and the API format are different files. A workflow saved from the browser UI is the UI
+format and will be rejected; use *Save (API Format)* or convert it.
+
+## Step 0 — what can this machine actually do
+
+Before installing anything or promising an image:
+
+```bash
+nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,memory.total,memory.used --format=csv,noheader 2>/dev/null \
+  || rocm-smi --showmeminfo vram 2>/dev/null \
+  || echo "no GPU"
+df -h /            # a checkpoint is 2–7 GB each; models fill a root in one afternoon
+```
+
+Rough VRAM floors (SDXL, 1024²): **6 GB** with `--lowvram`, **8 GB** comfortable, **12 GB** for batch or
+img2img chains. SD 1.5 runs on 4–6 GB. Video and upscale pipelines want far more. If the machine cannot do
+it, say so before the download — that is cheaper for everyone than a 20-minute install that OOMs.
+
+## Driving it
+
+```bash
+# health
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://127.0.0.1:8188/system_stats
+
+# what checkpoints are installed
+curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8188/object_info/CheckpointLoaderSimple | python3 -m json.tool | head -40
+
+# queue a workflow (API format)
+curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8188/prompt \
+  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
+  -d "{\"prompt\": $(cat workflow_api.json), \"client_id\": \"agent\"}"
+# -> {"prompt_id": "...", "number": N}
+```
+
+Then poll for the result rather than guessing a duration:
+
+```bash
+curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:8188/history/$PROMPT_ID" | python3 -m json.tool | head -40
+```
+
+Outputs land in the server's `output/` directory with the filename the save node chose; `/history` gives you
+the exact name plus the subfolder. Read the images from there — do not assume `ComfyUI_00001_.png`.
+
+**Progress beats polling** where the WebSocket is available: subscribe to
+`ws://127.0.0.1:8188/ws?clientId=agent` and read `progress` messages (`value`/`max`). It costs one
+connection and turns a blind wait into a real percentage.
+
+## Finding the knobs in a workflow you did not author
+
+A workflow JSON is opaque until you enumerate it. Every parameter worth changing is an input that is not a
+link:
+
+```python
+import json
+wf = json.load(open("workflow_api.json"))
+for nid, node in wf.items():
+    print(nid, node["class_type"])
+    for k, v in node.get("inputs", {}).items():
+        kind = "LINK<-" + str(v[0]) if isinstance(v, list) else repr(v)[:70]
+        print(f"   {k:24} {kind}")
+```
+
+The four you will nearly always touch: the **positive prompt** (`CLIPTextEncode.text` feeding the
+`KSampler.positive`), the **negative prompt**, the **seed** (`KSampler.seed`), and **steps / cfg /
+sampler_name / scheduler** on the same node. Resolution lives on an `EmptyLatentImage` node; batch size is
+`batch_size` on the same node.
+
+**Change only what the job needs.** A seed sweep is one edit per run; re-writing the whole graph between
+runs is how a working workflow turns into a broken one.
+
+## Prompt-craft that survives a local model
+
+- **Order matters**: subject → attributes → setting → lighting → medium/style. Front-load what must not be
+  lost.
+- **SDXL-class models ignore negatives less reliably than SD1.5** — a negative prompt is still worth a
+  short list of the three things you do not want, not a paragraph.
+- **Seeds are reproducible only with the same model, sampler, scheduler, steps and resolution.** Record all
+  of them next to any image you keep.
+- **Upscale rather than asking for a bigger canvas.** Generating at 1536² directly on a 12 GB card produces
+  duplicated limbs; generate at 1024² and run a latent or ESRGAN upscale.
+
+## Presenting results
+
+- Show the image, not a path to it.
+- Say which model, seed, steps and size produced it — one line. Without that, a good result is not
+  reproducible and a bad one is not diagnosable.
+- If a run failed because of a missing node or checkpoint, name the exact missing object; "the workflow did
+  not run" is not actionable.
+
+## Pitfalls
+
+- **A UI-format workflow cannot be POSTed.** Convert to API format first; the error otherwise is a
+  validation complaint about node ids.
+- **The server is single-queue.** Two POSTs queue in order; a "hung" run is often the one behind a large
+  job. Check `/queue` before assuming a failure.
+- **A missing custom node fails at queue time, not at load.** The error names the class; install the pack
+  and restart the server.
+- **Model files in the wrong folder are invisible.** Each loader reads one directory
+  (`models/checkpoints`, `models/loras`, `models/vae`, …). A 6 GB download in `models/` is not seen.
+- **First run after a restart is slow** — the model has to be loaded and compiled. Do not report that as
+  the workflow's speed.
+- **Do not drive a UI on someone's screen to do an API job.** If the server is reachable, use the API.
