+---
+name: linux-ops
+description: "Diagnose a Linux box that is out of space, full of stray processes, or failing units — measure first, attribute before acting."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [linux, systemd, storage, processes, disk, diagnostics]
+---
+
+# Linux ops: space, processes, units
+
+Three failures cover most "the machine is broken" reports on a Linux box: a filesystem filled by
+something nobody expected, a process nobody started, and a unit that has been failed since boot. All
+three are diagnosable in a few measured commands, and all three are routinely misdiagnosed because the
+first plausible theory was acted on before anything was measured.
+
+## Rule zero — measure before you delete anything
+
+`du`, `df`, `ps`, `systemctl status` cost seconds. A deletion costs a rebuild. Print the numbers you are
+acting on, then act.
+
+## Disk: root is full
+
+```bash
+df -hT                                     # per-filesystem, WITH type — a FUSE/NTFS mount is not root
+findmnt -o TARGET,SOURCE,FSTYPE,SIZE,USED,AVAIL
+```
+
+The type column matters more than the numbers: a 4 TB NTFS mount can sit at 297 MB/s while the same
+class of file on the local btrfs reads at 2.7 GB/s, and a full `ntfs-3g` mount behaves oddly rather than
+erroring cleanly.
+
+Find the consumers, largest first, in one pass:
+
+```bash
+du -x -h --max-depth=1 / 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -20
+# then drill where the weight is:
+du -x -h --max-depth=1 /home 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -20
+```
+
+`-x` stops the traversal crossing into other filesystems — without it a remote mount drags the scan to a
+crawl and reports someone else's numbers.
+
+The usual repeat offenders, in order:
+
+- **Package and build caches** — `~/.cargo`, `~/.cache/uv`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cache/pip`, `~/.cache/huggingface`.
+  A single `~/.cache/uv` has been seen at 13 GB on a 96 GB root.
+- **Container and VM images** — `docker system df`, then `docker system prune` (say what it will delete).
+- **Journal** — `journalctl --disk-usage`, then `journalctl --vacuum-size=500M`.
+- **Snapshots** — `btrfs filesystem usage /`, `snapper list`, `timeshift --list`. Snapshots share
+  extents, so deleting the wrong one frees nothing.
+- **Model and media files** — multi-GB by nature, and the ones that fill a root every time.
+
+**Prove whether anything is still writing before you clean up.** A filesystem that collapses back to
+zero shortly after you free space is being filled by a live process:
+
+```bash
+find / -xdev -newermt '-10 minutes' -type f -size +50M 2>/dev/null | head
+```
+
+**Move, verify, then link.** For anything large crossing filesystems, `cp` then compare (`sha256sum`,
+or size + a content probe), then remove the source, then symlink back. Never `mv` a large tree and
+assume it arrived.
+
+**Long copies are background jobs with a real progress check.** Start them, report the start, and check
+`ps`/size growth when asked — do not narrate or poll on a timer. A cross-filesystem `mv` keeps running
+after a shell timeout, which is useful to know and misleading if you assume it died.
+
+### A mount that is present but has no free space
+
+Check inode exhaustion as well as bytes: `df -i`. A filesystem at 1 % used with 0 inodes free cannot
+create a file either, and it reads as a permissions error from most applications.
+
+## Processes: what is this, who started it, why is it back
+
+```bash
+ps -eo pid,ppid,pcpu,pmem,etime,wchan:20,cmd --sort=-pcpu | head -20
+ss -ltnp                      # who owns a listening port (needs privileges for foreign processes)
+```
+
+**Attribute a port to a unit before deciding what it is:**
+
+```bash
+ss -ltnp | grep ':8086'
+systemctl --user status <maybe-matching-unit>
+systemctl status <maybe-matching-unit>
+```
+
+Two listeners on adjacent ports is a normal state, and the wrong one owning a port explains a service
+that "works" at 25 s per request. Measure the response time before theorising:
+
+```bash
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{time_total}\n' http://127.0.0.1:PORT/health
+```
+
+**A process that will not die is usually a unit respawning it.** `kill <pid>` then watching it return
+with a new pid is the signature:
+
+```bash
+systemctl status <unit>          # look for "Restart=" and the restart counter
+systemctl stop <unit>            # stop the UNIT, then verify the pid is gone
+```
+
+Never leave a service stopped without saying so — something may be mid-conversation on it.
+
+**Hung, or just parked?** `wchan`, the CPU delta over two samples, and whether it holds the resource it
+is waiting on:
+
+```bash
+cat /proc/<pid>/wchan; echo
+awk '{print $14+$15}' /proc/<pid>/stat; sleep 2; awk '{print $14+$15}' /proc/<pid>/stat
+ls -l /proc/<pid>/fd | wc -l     # a descriptor count that grows is a leak, not a wait
+```
+
+## Units: a failure that has been failing since boot
+
+```bash
+systemctl --failed
+systemctl status <unit> -l --no-pager
+journalctl --user-unit <unit> -n 60 --no-pager
+```
+
+**Read the status code before the theory.** `status=203/EXEC` is a missing or non-executable binary;
+`status=1/FAILURE` is the program's own exit code; `status=226/NAMESPACE` is a sandbox path that does
+not exist; `code=killed, signal=SEGV` is a crash. Each one rules out whole classes of cause, and guessing
+without reading it produces "restart the service" answers that never hold.
+
+**A systemd user unit gets no session environment.** `WAYLAND_DISPLAY`, `DISPLAY`,
+`DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`, `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` are all absent — that is by design, not a bug. Lift them
+from the live session when the unit needs them:
+
+```bash
+pid=$(pgrep -x plasmashell | head -1)
+tr '\0' '\n' < /proc/$pid/environ | grep -E '^(WAYLAND_DISPLAY|DISPLAY|DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS|XDG_RUNTIME_DIR)='
+```
+
+and re-read them on every start, because the Wayland socket renumbers across reboots (`wayland-0` →
+`wayland-1`).
+
+**A restart loop is a population problem, not a crash.** A unit with `Restart=always` that dies after 9 s
+looks like a leak and is usually two instances of the same thing fighting for one resource — a model
+loading twice into one GPU, two daemons on one port, a lock file. Read what *else* is running before
+treating it as a memory bug.
+
+## Rules that keep earning their place
+
+- **Never sweep every machine for a problem reported on one.** The box named is the blast radius; a
+  fleet-wide change for a single complaint is damage, and the revert is what gets remembered.
+- **Do not restart or stop anything a person might be using without saying so first.** Say what stops,
+  for how long, and what comes back.
+- **A "please reboot" without a root cause is not a fix.** Read the journal for the specific error, fix
+  that, then verify the fix addresses it — one reboot per hypothesis at most.
+- **Check `/proc` before assuming a binary is gone.** A deleted-but-running binary is recoverable from
+  `/proc/<pid>/exe`, which has saved a wiped `~/.local/bin` more than once.
+- **`pgrep -f '<pattern>'` matches the shell running it.** Use `pgrep -x` or read `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`
+  when the pattern appears in your own command line.
