+---
+name: local-inference
+description: "Run and inspect local GGUF models: the container format, quant math, llama.cpp serving flags, and how to benchmark without lying to yourself."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [llama.cpp, gguf, quantization, inference, benchmark, vram]
+---
+
+# Local inference and GGUF
+
+## The container
+
+A GGUF is a header, a tensor table, and a data blob. You can read the whole table without downloading the
+weights:
+
+```
+magic "GGUF" | version | tensor_count | kv_count | key-value metadata | tensor infos | alignment pad | data
+```
+
+Each tensor info carries name, n_dims, dims[], type, and an **offset relative to the data section start**
+— not to the file start. Getting that base wrong reads plausible garbage, which is the worst failure mode
+because it does not error.
+
+**Parse the header before you trust any tool's summary.** A GGUF whose `general.architecture` disagrees
+with its filename is common (a mislabelled image model that is actually an LLM, a "27B" that is a 35B
+MoE); the header settles it in one read.
+
+## Quant layouts worth knowing cold
+
+| type | block | bytes/block | notes |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| F16 / BF16 | 1 | 2 | reference |
+| Q8_0 | 32 | 34 | one fp16 scale per block |
+| Q5_K | 256 | 176 | super-block with 6-bit scales and mins, 5th bit packed |
+| Q4_K | 256 | 144 | `d(2) dmin(2) scales[12] qs[128]`; 8 sub-blocks of 32 |
+
+Q4_K dequantises as `d * scale * q - dmin * min`, with the low nibbles in `qs[0..63]` and the high nibbles
+in `qs[64..127]`. The fp16→fp32 conversion is the ggml bit-twiddle, **not** textbook IEEE expansion — port
+it exactly or every value is subtly wrong.
+
+## Doing the memory arithmetic before choosing a quant
+
+Bytes moved per token, not file size, is the number that predicts speed.
+
+```
+dense model:   bytes/token ≈ file_size_bytes                     (every weight read per token)
+MoE model:     bytes/token ≈ backbone + (top_k / n_experts) × expert_bytes
+```
+
+A worked example that surprises people: in one 110 GB MoE, the 5.5 GB backbone was re-read every token
+while the 29 GB expert bank only contributed ~1.5 GB/token — so the *small* part was four times the
+traffic. The conclusion: **quantise the always-hot part first.** Dropping the backbone from 8.5 to 4.5
+bits saved 2.6 GB/token; dropping every expert from 4.5 to 2.6 saved 0.6.
+
+Then divide by measured bandwidth, not spec-sheet bandwidth:
+
+```
+ms/token ≈ bytes_per_token / (bytes_per_second achievable)
+```
+
+Measure the achievable number on the actual mount. On the same machine, one NVMe partition served
+**2.7 GB/s** cold while an `ntfs-3g` (FUSE) mount of another drive served **297 MB/s** — a 9× difference
+that looks exactly like "the model is slow" and is really "the model is on the wrong filesystem".
+
+## Serving
+
+```bash
+llama-server -m model.gguf \
+  -ngl 99 \                 # layers on GPU; a partial value is a compromise, not a mistake
+  --ctx-size 32768 \
+  -fa \                     # flash attention where supported
+  --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080
+```
+
+- **Bind to loopback** unless something on the network genuinely needs it.
+- **`--parallel` decides your KV cache and your latency.** With `--parallel 1` there is one slot, and any
+  other request — including a tiny health probe — evicts the cached prefix of the conversation in flight.
+  Every real turn then re-prefills. This is the usual cause of "it went slow again for no reason".
+- **Check `/slots` for cache reuse**, not just `/health`:
+  `n_prompt_tokens_cache / n_prompt_tokens` is the fraction of the prompt that did not need recomputation.
+  A healthy multi-turn session sits high; 0 % every turn means something is invalidating the prefix.
+- **Prompt caching matches the longest common token prefix.** Anything inserted *near the front* of the
+  prompt (a recall block glued into the system message, a timestamp, a shuffled tool list) invalidates
+  everything after it. Put per-turn injected content at the **end of the last user message**.
+- **Reasoning models spend completion tokens before they emit anything.** A `max_tokens` that is too small
+  returns `finish_reason: length` with empty content — which looks identical to a refusal. Set a generous
+  budget (≥ 12000 for a long task), lower the reasoning effort if the runtime supports it, and **always log
+  `finish_reason`**.
+
+## Benchmarking without lying to yourself
+
+- **Warm up first.** Page cache is worth an 8× swing on the same model: one measured run went from 375 ms
+  to 47 ms per token purely from being warm. A cold number is not a rate.
+- **A/B inside one process, alternating.** Cross-process comparison lets a one-time cost (uploading
+  weights, page faults) swamp a real 10–20 % win, and it has hidden real wins.
+- **Repeated single-token runs are a best case**, not an average: the same experts fire every step. Report
+  prose generation and best-case separately.
+- **Report the floor you are at.** If `bytes/token ÷ measured bandwidth` predicts 200 ms and you measure
+  205 ms, there is nothing left to win inside that design — say so instead of continuing to tune.
+- **`perf` beats theories** where it is available (needs `kernel.perf_event_paranoid` ≤ 1). Beware that it
+  attributes time to page faults that may not cost wall clock.
+- **Measure the thing you changed.** Two builds, one variable, one run each, same warm state.
+
+## Choosing a quant for a machine
+
+1. Compute the full-resident size; if it exceeds RAM, stop treating "runs" as a yes/no question.
+2. Decide what must be resident: backbone, KV cache, and the active expert set.
+3. KV size: `2 × n_layers × n_kv_heads × head_dim × ctx × bytes_per_element`. Quantising KV to q8 costs
+   ~half and is usually free in quality; **budget it before raising context**, because context is the
+   thing that silently zeroes your headroom.
+4. Only then pick a file, and prefer the one whose *hot* part is smallest rather than whose file is
+   smallest.
+
+## Pitfalls
+
+- Reading a tensor offset relative to file start instead of the data section.
+- Assuming file size predicts speed on a Mixture-of-Experts model.
+- Loading weights from a FUSE/NTFS mount and concluding the GPU is slow.
+- Reporting a cold-start rate as throughput.
+- Trusting a filename for the architecture; parse `general.architecture`.
+- Forgetting that a second server process on the same GPU will not fail cleanly — it will take what it can
+  get and silently degrade both.
