+---
+name: memory-hygiene
+description: "Write memory that survives: which layer a fact belongs in, what to record, and how to reconstruct a span of work without trusting your own recall."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [memory, context, notes, session, continuity, journal]
+---
+
+# Memory hygiene
+
+An agent with no memory re-derives the same conclusions every session. An agent with bad memory is worse:
+it carries confident, stale claims and acts on them. The difference is not storage capacity, it is knowing
+which layer a fact belongs in and never writing a fact you did not verify.
+
+## The layers, and what each one is for
+
+| Layer | Holds | Cost of getting it wrong |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Identity / instructions** | who the agent is, standing rules, the user's preferences | every turn reads it — bloat slows everything and buries the important line |
+| **Durable facts** | stable environment facts, conventions, who the user is | repeated as fact forever; a wrong entry outlives the session that wrote it |
+| **Procedures (skills)** | how to do a recurring *kind* of task | loads only when relevant; a stale procedure executes confidently and wrongly |
+| **Verbatim store** | committed records, transcripts, decisions | nobody re-reads it wholesale — it is for reconstruction |
+| **Session history** | what happened, in what order | searchable, never injected |
+
+**Facts that apply to every session go in the durable layer. Anything learned while doing a task —
+procedures, pitfalls, corrections for a category of work — belongs in the procedure, where it loads only
+when relevant.** Putting a task lesson into the always-injected layer costs every future turn and is
+re-read as a standing instruction.
+
+## Write a fact as a fact
+
+- **Declarative, not imperative.** "The user prefers short answers" ✓. "Always answer briefly" ✗ — the
+  second is re-read later as a directive and can override what the user is asking for *now*.
+- **A correction is a fact about preferences, not a mood.** Record the preference, never the emotional
+  read that produced it.
+- **Record live state from a measurement.** A port number, a model id, an IP, a mount type — read it, then
+  write it. State copied from an earlier note is how stale configs propagate for months.
+- **Date the things that will move.** A version, a price, a running service: write when you measured it.
+- **Never write a credential into a note.** Store a path or a command that retrieves it.
+
+## What is worth keeping
+
+Keep things that are (a) expensive to rediscover, (b) stable for weeks, and (c) not already obvious from
+the environment. That is roughly: environment topology, the user's standing preferences, hard-won
+tool traps, decisions with a reason attached.
+
+Skip: task progress, completed-work logs, anything re-discoverable in one command, and anything that will
+be false by Friday. If it is a procedure, it is a skill, not a memory.
+
+## Reconstructing a span of work
+
+When asked "where were we", the order matters — use the **first source that answers, never the last**:
+
+1. **Live state on disk** — the file, the process, the unit, the git log. Authoritative.
+2. **The committed record** — decision notes, changelogs, the last handoff.
+3. **Session history** — search it by topic, then read the turns around the hit.
+4. **Your own recollection** — last resort, and labelled as such.
+
+**Digest, never dump.** A reconstruction is 10–20 lines: what was decided, what was done, what was
+measured, what is open, what is uncertain. Pasting a transcript back at someone is not a summary.
+
+**Mark what you did not re-verify — but establish reachability first.** "I did not re-run the tests" is an
+honest label. "I could not find the file" is a different claim; check the path exists before reporting on
+its contents.
+
+**Verify the mechanism, not the message.** A note saying "the service is running" is not evidence; the
+port answering is.
+
+## The append-only journal
+
+A per-turn journal — one line per action, appended, never rewritten — is the cheapest continuity
+insurance there is:
+
+```
+2026-05-02T14:03Z  START    rebuild of the parser started, 12 files
+2026-05-02T14:41Z  DONE     11 of 12 pass, one fixture missing
+2026-05-02T14:42Z  BLOCKED  needs the fixture from upstream, asked
+```
+
+- **Append only.** Rewriting history to look tidier destroys the one property that makes a journal useful.
+- **Timestamp every line from a real clock**, and never state a duration you did not compute from two
+  timestamps.
+- **Heartbeat separately from work.** "Still alive" and "still progressing" are different signals, and a
+  long job should say which one it is emitting.
+- **One line, not a paragraph.** The journal is scanned, not read.
+
+## Structure that scales
+
+- **Flat key + value beats nested.** A memory that needs parsing to read is a memory that will be misread.
+- **Every entry stands alone.** Entries get loaded out of order and out of context; one that begins "as
+  noted above" is lost.
+- **Prune by replacing, not by appending.** When a note is superseded, patch it. Two conflicting notes on
+  the same topic will eventually be read as two facts.
+- **Keep the always-loaded layer small.** If it has grown, the answer is to move detail into the
+  procedures it belongs to, not to compress it into shorthand nobody can parse.
+
+## The failure modes to watch for
+
+- **Confident recall of a thing that changed.** Check the live source before asserting.
+- **A memory that restates the instruction as a personality trait.** "User is angry" is a label; "user
+  swears as cadence and is not angry by default" is the useful version — and the first one will shape
+  every future answer wrongly.
+- **Duplicating a rule in three layers** so that updating one leaves two stale copies.
+- **Summarising the summary.** After a few rounds of "what we discussed", the record drifts from the
+  events. Go back to the source turn.
