+---
+name: multi-machine-offload
+description: "Put a second computer to work over SSH: offload a job, reach a service that is bound to loopback, and tell what a remote box is actually doing."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [ssh, offload, fleet, remote, tunnels, gpu]
+---
+
+# Working across more than one machine
+
+The value of a second box is not raw power, it is a different resource pool: the GPU that is free, the
+disk that is not full, the CPU that is not mid-build. The cost is that every assumption you make about it
+is probably stale.
+
+## Before anything: what is that box doing right now
+
+One SSH call, one answer — do not ask three questions in three round trips:
+
+```bash
+ssh "$BOX" 'hostname; uptime -p; \
+  free -g | awk "/Mem:/{print \"mem \"\$3\"/\"\$2\" GB\"}"; \
+  nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,memory.used,memory.total,utilization.gpu --format=csv,noheader 2>/dev/null; \
+  df -h / /home 2>/dev/null | tail -2; \
+  systemctl --user list-units --type=service --state=running --no-legend | wc -l'
+```
+
+Read the numbers before assigning work. A box with the GPU already holding 19 GB is not the box for a
+20 GB model, and a box at 98 % on `/` cannot accept output.
+
+## Offloading a job
+
+```bash
+ssh "$BOX" 'cd /path/to/work && nohup ./long-job > run.log 2>&1 & echo $!'
+# later
+ssh "$BOX" 'ps -o pid,etime,pcpu,rss,cmd -p <pid>; tail -5 run.log'
+```
+
+- **Redirect everything.** An unredirected background process dies with the SSH session, and the log you
+  needed goes with it.
+- **Write to a path you can retrieve**, and print that path in the result — not a path that only exists in
+  the remote shell's cwd.
+- **Check it is progressing, not just alive**: CPU delta across two samples, log tail, output file growth.
+  A process parked in a poll loop burns no CPU and produces nothing; `wchan` and the output mtime separate
+  "working" from "spinning".
+- **Long jobs are background jobs with a real end.** Report the start, then check when asked or when it
+  finishes. Narrating each step is noise.
+
+## Reaching a service bound to loopback
+
+Most sane services bind `127.0.0.1`. That is a feature; do not change it. Tunnel to it:
+
+```bash
+ssh -N -L 8080:127.0.0.1:8080 "$BOX" &     # then talk to http://127.0.0.1:8080 locally
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://127.0.0.1:8080/health
+```
+
+The tunnel is the right answer far more often than re-binding the service to `0.0.0.0`. Two caveats: pick
+a local port that is genuinely free, and remember the tunnel dies with the shell that opened it — a
+`nohup`ed tunnel is a leaked process you will later mistake for a service.
+
+**A relay is the fallback for a client that cannot tunnel** (a phone, a browser, an app with one URL
+field): a small TCP relay on the box that *can* reach the service, forwarding a port. Dumb, stateless,
+one process, and it must be documented where the client's config lives.
+
+## Two servers, one card
+
+Two processes can both start on one GPU and neither will fail cleanly. The second one gets whatever is
+left and both run degraded, which reads as "the model got slower" rather than "there are two models".
+Before blaming the code:
+
+```bash
+nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps=pid,process_name,used_memory --format=csv
+```
+
+If it is a managed setup, use a **mutually exclusive group** so the manager unloads one before loading the
+other. If it is not managed, stop the one you are not using — and say that you did.
+
+## Environment differences that bite
+
+- **No session environment over SSH.** `WAYLAND_DISPLAY`, `DISPLAY`, `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS` and
+  `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` are absent, so anything GUI or D-Bus fails in a way that looks like a missing binary.
+  Lift them from the live session when needed.
+- **`sudo` differs per box.** Never assume a password, a rule, or a key is the same. Test: `sudo -n true`.
+- **A different Python.** The interpreter on `PATH` may not be the one with the dependency. Say which one
+  you used: `head -1 $(which python3)`, `python3 -c 'import sys; print(sys.executable)'`.
+- **A different CPU feature set.** Code compiled `-march=native` on one box is not portable to the other —
+  and a build that runs but produces wrong numbers (rather than crashing) is the worst version of this.
+- **A different filesystem.** A FUSE/NTFS mount can be 9× slower than the local disk on the same class of
+  file. Check `findmnt -o TARGET,FSTYPE` before deciding a job is CPU-bound.
+
+## Naming and addressing
+
+- **Keep a table of which box is which**, with what each one is for (main GPU, CPU worker, training node,
+  low-power node). Ten lines, saved, beats reconstructing it from `~/.ssh/config` every session.
+- **A stale alias is a real bug.** If a host's address changed, update every reference at once — the SSH
+  config, any relay, any kernel command line, any documentation. Half-updated addressing produces failures
+  that look random.
+- **Say which machine ran the work.** A result with no provenance gets attributed to the wrong box later,
+  and the wrong box gets debugged.
+
+## Cost model, so the split is not arbitrary
+
+Put the job where the *bottleneck* is cheapest:
+
+| bottleneck | send it to |
+|---|---|
+| GPU compute or VRAM | the box with a free card |
+| RAM, page cache | the box with the most memory per byte of working set |
+| sustained disk read | the box with the fastest local filesystem — never a FUSE mount |
+| long-running CPU grind | any box, but one that will not be needed for anything else |
+| interactive latency | the box the user is actually sitting at |
