+---
+name: post-media-read
+description: "Read a social post someone pasted — text, thread, and the media itself — with no account and no login wall."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [x, twitter, syndication, media, ocr, vision]
+---
+
+# Reading a pasted post
+
+A pasted link is a request to read it. "No content extracted" almost always means you used the wrong
+endpoint, not that the post is gone.
+
+## The one-liner
+
+For X/Twitter, the syndication endpoint answers plain HTTP with no auth, no cookies, and no browser:
+
+```bash
+ID=1234567890123456789
+curl -s "https://cdn.syndication.twimg.com/tweet-result?id=$ID&lang=en&token=0" -o /tmp/post.json
+python3 - <<'PY'
+import json
+d = json.load(open('/tmp/post.json'))
+print(d.get('user', {}).get('screen_name'), '|', d.get('created_at'))
+print(d.get('text', ''))
+for m in (d.get('mediaDetails') or []):
+    print(m.get('type'), m.get('media_url_https'))
+PY
+```
+
+The post id is the number after `/status/`. Get the handle from the same URL — it is only used for the
+alternative endpoints and for quoting the source correctly.
+
+## The syndication payload is truncated, not complete
+
+Long posts ("notes") come back cut off, with no marker saying so. When the text looks like it stops
+mid-sentence, or the account is known for long-form:
+
+```bash
+curl -s "https://api.fxtwitter.com/<handle>/status/$ID" | python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(d["tweet"]["text"])'
+```
+
+**Check the returned id matches the one you asked for** before quoting — a redirect or a stale cache
+hands back a different post, and the mistake is invisible once the text is pasted into an answer.
+
+`api.vxtwitter.com` returns 403 on the same URL; do not route through it. `oEmbed` is the third fallback
+when both of the above are unhappy — it gives the author and a short excerpt, which is enough to identify
+the post even when the body is unavailable.
+
+## The media IS the content
+
+When the post's substance is an image — a screenshot of a document, a chart, a receipt, a settings panel,
+a chart axis, a table — the text of the post is caption, and answering from it is answering from the
+caption.
+
+1. Take `media_url_https` and **append `?name=orig`**. The default URL is a downscale, and the downscale
+   is exactly where fine print, small type and axis labels die.
+2. Download it to a scratch path, then read it with the vision tool at full resolution.
+3. **Ask for the text verbatim**, not for a summary. "Transcribe every line" produces something you can
+   check; "what does this say" produces a paraphrase you cannot.
+4. For a dense crop, re-crop the region and ask again — accuracy on small type improves sharply when the
+   image is cropped to the text rather than scaled whole.
+
+Video: variants appear in either of two shapes in the payload — `video.variants[]` with
+`type`/`src` (pick `type == "video/mp4"`) or `mediaDetails[].video_info.variants[]` with
+`content_type`/`url`. Check both; a single check "finds no video" on perfectly good posts.
+
+## A post that quotes a number is a secondary source
+
+Screenshots of statistics circulate with the axis cropped, the baseline changed, or the period omitted. If
+the number matters, find the original: the agency release, the filing, the vendor's own chart. Quote the
+post and the primary source separately, and say which is which.
+
+## Posts that are gated
+
+An age-check or "sensitive content" interstitial is a **server-side withhold** for these endpoints: the
+media was never sent. Do not spend calls trying to unblur it.
+
+Diagnose it in one step: if the HTML loads and the images have `naturalWidth === 0` with zero requests to
+the site's own media endpoint in `performance.getEntriesByType('resource')`, the bytes do not exist in
+your session. The only honest options are (a) report that the media is behind a gate, or (b) fetch it with
+a session that is entitled to it, in a separate browser profile — never the user's live profile.
+
+## Threads
+
+Every payload carries a `parent` — walk it upward to reconstruct the thread instead of guessing at the
+conversation from one post:
+
+```bash
+python3 - <<'PY'
+import json, subprocess
+cur, out = "<ID>", []
+while cur:
+    d = json.loads(subprocess.run(["curl","-s",
+        f"https://cdn.syndication.twimg.com/tweet-result?id={cur}&lang=en&token=0"],
+        capture_output=True, text=True).stdout or "{}")
+    if not d.get("text"): break
+    out.append((d.get("user",{}).get("screen_name"), d["text"]))
+    cur = (d.get("parent") or {}).get("id_str")
+for who, text in reversed(out):
+    print(f"{who}: {text}\n")
+PY
+```
+
+## Searching without a browser
+
+For a feed, a keyword or an account's posts, a syndication-style timeline endpoint or a search engine
+query scoped to the domain (`site:x.com <term>`) beats driving the site: x.com itself is a JavaScript
+application that returns a shell to any plain HTTP client, and its logged-out view is deliberately
+degraded. Reading a *pasted post* is a solved problem; browsing the site is not, and does not need to be.
+
+## Verification before quoting
+
+- The id in the payload equals the id in the URL.
+- The handle equals the handle in the URL.
+- The text ends in punctuation, or you retrieved the long-form version.
+- If you read an image, you can point at where in the image the claim came from.
