+---
+name: terminal-email
+description: "Read, search, draft and send email from the shell over IMAP/SMTP — with the flag-order and folder-name traps that make commands appear to succeed silently."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [email, imap, smtp, himalaya, cli]
+---
+
+# Email from the terminal
+
+A CLI mail client (himalaya is the reference here) talks IMAP for reading and SMTP for sending, so it
+works over any provider that offers both — including ones with no API. The traps are almost all about
+folder names and argument order, and they all fail in the same quiet way: a command that looks like it
+worked and did nothing.
+
+## Check the major version before anything else
+
+```bash
+himalaya --version
+```
+
+v1 and v2 take *different flags*, and a v1 command against a v2 binary fails with a usage error that
+reads like a config problem. Write every example below against the version you actually have.
+
+## Configuration
+
+`~/.config/himalaya/config.toml` — one `[accounts.<name>]` block per mailbox:
+
+```toml
+[accounts.main]
+email = "you@example.com"
+display-name = "Your Name"
+
+backend.type = "imap"
+backend.host = "imap.example.com"
+backend.port = 993
+backend.encryption.type = "tls"
+backend.login = "you@example.com"
+backend.auth.type = "password"
+backend.auth.cmd = "pass show mail/example"     # or a keyring command — never a literal password
+
+message.send.backend.type = "smtp"
+message.send.backend.host = "smtp.example.com"
+message.send.backend.port = 465
+message.send.backend.encryption.type = "tls"
+message.send.backend.login = "you@example.com"
+message.send.backend.auth.type = "password"
+message.send.backend.auth.cmd = "pass show mail/example"
+```
+
+**Keep the secret out of the file.** An `auth.cmd` that reads a password manager or a key file is the
+only shape that survives a dotfile repo.
+
+**Folder aliases are not optional.** Servers use their own names, and the client's canonical names
+(`inbox`, `sent`, `drafts`, `trash`) silently resolve to nothing when they do not match:
+
+```toml
+folder.aliases.inbox  = "INBOX"
+folder.aliases.sent   = "[Gmail]/Sent Mail"
+folder.aliases.drafts = "[Gmail]/Drafts"
+folder.aliases.trash  = "[Gmail]/Trash"
+```
+
+Get the real names first — `himalaya folder list` — then alias every one you will touch. This is the
+single most common cause of "the message was sent but Sent is empty".
+
+## Reading
+
+```bash
+himalaya envelope list                          # newest first; a page at a time
+himalaya envelope list -f INBOX -s 20
+himalaya message read <ID>                      # by envelope id from the listing
+himalaya message export <ID> --full > msg.eml   # keep the raw message, headers included
+```
+
+**Put every option before the query string.** In v2, `envelope search` treats trailing words as the
+query; an option placed after the query is parsed as search text and the command returns a plausible,
+wrong, empty set:
+
+```bash
+himalaya envelope search --subject "invoice" --from billing@  subject invoice   # options first
+```
+
+**Gmail's sent mail is not where you think.** Depending on version and aliasing, your own outgoing mail
+is most reliably read from `[Gmail]/All Mail` or from `INBOX` (Gmail's server-side copy), not from the
+sent folder path you assumed.
+
+## Sending
+
+```bash
+# draft to a file first — this is the default for anything with consequences
+cat > /tmp/reply.eml <<'EOF'
+From: you@example.com
+To: someone@example.com
+Subject: Re: the thing
+In-Reply-To: <original-message-id@host>
+
+Body text.
+EOF
+
+himalaya message send < /tmp/reply.eml              # v2: reads a full RFC 5322 message from stdin
+himalaya template send < /tmp/reply.eml             # v1 equivalent
+```
+
+**Attachments go through a file, not a flag chain.** Build the `.eml` (base64 the part, set
+`Content-Type` and `Content-Disposition`) and pass the path. Long `--attachment` flag chains quote badly
+and fail after the send has already been committed.
+
+**Drafting is the default mode.** Writing a message to a file and showing it is a draft. Sending happens
+only when asked for explicitly — a message that leaves the machine cannot be recalled, and "I drafted it"
+and "I sent it" are not the same verb.
+
+## Verify a send from a different path
+
+A `0` exit from the send command means the SMTP transaction was accepted, not that the message is
+readable in the mailbox. Confirm it where a human would see it:
+
+```bash
+himalaya envelope list -f "[Gmail]/All Mail" | head
+```
+
+IMAP sync is not real-time: check once, then once more after a few seconds, and stop. Polling a mailbox
+a dozen times reads as broken, not thorough. If the provider's copy has not appeared but the send was
+accepted, say that — it is a sync lag, not a failure.
+
+## Reading mail as evidence
+
+When the mailbox is the source of truth for a decision (a receipt, an approval, a termination, a court
+notice):
+
+- **Export the raw message and keep it.** `--full` preserves headers, which is what makes a message
+  quotable; a rendered body loses the `Date`, the `Message-ID` and every `Received` hop.
+- **Search broadly, then narrow.** `[Gmail]/All Mail` catches things a folder-scoped search misses,
+  including mail filed by a rule you forgot existed.
+- **Quote the sender and the date, not your summary of them.**
+- **Do not assume a decision arrived by email.** Some institutions send paper or portal-only notices;
+  an empty mailbox is not a negative decision.
+
+## Debugging
+
+```bash
+RUST_LOG=debug himalaya envelope list -f INBOX 2>&1 | tail -40
+```
+
+Read the protocol trace, not the summary line. An authentication failure, a TLS failure and a folder that
+does not exist all surface as "cannot list envelopes" at the default log level, and only the trace
+separates them.
+
+## Pitfalls
+
+- **A password in `config.toml` is a leak.** Use `auth.cmd`.
+- **An unaliased folder silently resolves to nothing.** List the server's folders first.
+- **Options after the query string are parsed as query text.** Order matters in v2.
+- **A successful SMTP exit is not a readable message.** Verify from the mailbox, once.
+- **`--json` output is versioned too.** Parse defensively and print the raw value when a field is
+  missing, rather than reporting a default.
