+---
+name: verification-discipline
+description: "Prove the artifact works before saying it does — the false failures, the false successes, and what 'verified' is allowed to mean."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [testing, verification, debugging, evidence, discipline]
+---
+
+# Verification discipline
+
+A checker that reports a defect is not evidence of a defect. A green light is not evidence of a working
+service. This skill exists because both of those sentences cost real hours.
+
+## The rule that resolves most of it
+
+**When a checker disagrees with reality, suspect the checker first — and say which one you are
+doubting.**
+
+The artifact is usually fine and the instrument is broken. Before you change working code to satisfy a
+failing check, prove the check can produce a TRUE POSITIVE: feed it the exact thing it is supposed to
+detect and watch it fail for the right reason. A check that has never failed once is not a check.
+
+## False failures — patterns that have all been seen
+
+1. **A matcher that also matches a different element.** A selector meant for one component type picked
+   up two other types in the same document and reported the good one as broken. Print what matched.
+2. **A regex too narrow for the values it must accept.** Rejects leading signs, thousands separators,
+   unicode minus, or the localised decimal comma.
+3. **Comparing raw text when the artifact normalises it.** Curly quotes, collapsed whitespace, ligatures
+   — diff the two strings and print both with their ordinals.
+4. **A matcher that hits the index before the content.** Table of contents, navigation, or summary hits
+   first, so the "content" found is a link list.
+5. **A vision model asked to inspect an image invents defects** — and it invents them plausibly. A small
+   local model is the *worst* checker: it hallucinates confidently about layout, colours and text. Trust
+   vision on a contained render (a chart, a crop); verify layout programmatically (pixel sampling,
+   bounding boxes, DOM geometry) and let vision only confirm.
+6. **The verifier's own arithmetic.** Off-by-one in a crop box, a scale factor applied twice (a 2×
+   screenshot has 2× coordinates), a sample origin at the wrong corner.
+7. **The sampling band excludes the signal.** A whole-page scan of a rendered document always hits the
+   header and footer rules; the number you wanted is in the middle. Skip the furniture zones and say so.
+8. **The diagnostic prints a span without its length** — a "match" of zero characters looks identical to
+   a match.
+9. **A symbol-name check is a false negative on an optimised build.** Inlined-away functions leave no
+   symbol. Test behaviour, not names.
+10. **A test that fails after your change is not evidence your change caused it.** Stash the change, run
+    the test again, then attribute. Cheap, and it prevents a wrong root cause.
+11. **The command you ran to check is itself a checker.** A `grep` whose pattern is wrong reports zero
+    hits; a `ps` through a shell that eats the pattern matches the shell's own command line. Print the
+    command, then the result.
+12. **A parsed field that comes back `0` or missing is a parse bug until proven otherwise.** Print the
+    source's own value set before filtering on it. A vocabulary mismatch (the data says `Aye`, the filter
+    tests for `yea`) silently inverts the whole result set.
+13. **The environment cannot exercise the capability**, so the failure is not about the artifact. No
+    compositor, no display, no permission — say so instead of "the code is broken".
+
+## False successes — the ones that ship broken
+
+- **Listed is not applied.** Reading the configuration back proves the file was written, not that the
+  running process took it. Restart, then re-measure the effect.
+- **A green health check is not a working service.** A socket file is not a daemon; a `200 /health` is
+  not the feature. Exercise the real path once, end to end.
+- **A content script that *loads* is not a fix that *works*.** Confirm the observable behaviour changed.
+- **A build that compiles clean is not a compliant build.** If the standard is "no allocations", compiling
+  proves nothing — measure allocations per unit of work and report the number.
+- **A job that exits 0 has not necessarily produced anything.** Count the artifacts, open one, check it is
+  not a stub. Report the count.
+- **A test that passes in isolation may pass *because* it is in isolation.** A suite that opens its
+  fixture and asserts immediately cannot distinguish per-session from per-call state.
+
+## What "verified" is allowed to mean
+
+Write it as a claim with a receipt, not an adjective:
+
+> ✓ 58 tests passed  ·  ✗ 3 of 61 failed (names)  ·  unverified (no hardware)
+
+- **A count and a name.** "tests pass" is not a receipt; "12 passed, 0 failed" is.
+- **Name what was not verified.** An honest unverified line beats a fabricated pass. Never substitute a
+  plausible-looking result for one you could not produce.
+- **Only the thing you actually ran.** Running the real path once beats a harness that simulates it —
+  especially when the user says "go": that means run the real thing and show the log.
+- **A declared total is an assertion.** If your output says "12 of 12", the enumerable count must agree.
+  When it does not, re-fetch or count programmatically; never finalise on "go with what I have".
+
+## Make the check a gate, not a printout
+
+A verification step that reports and continues is decoration. Failure must abort the pipeline and leave
+no artifact behind — no package, no image, no commit. And the gate must run against a **fresh,
+reconstructed** input every time, not the working tree: a chain that reproduces the tree is the only
+proof that what ships is what was tested.
+
+## Procedure for a check that fails
+
+1. Re-read the check. Is it reading the artifact or a summary of the artifact?
+2. Reproduce the failure by hand, in one command, and read the raw output.
+3. Make the check fail deliberately on a known-bad input. Does it report the right reason?
+4. If the check is sound, change **one** parameter and re-test — never two.
+5. If the failure repeats with the same shape, the instrument is wrong; change class of instrument, not
+   the query, the seed, or the timing.
