+---
+name: voice-pipeline
+description: "Wire speech in and out of an agent that runs locally: TTS to the right sink, STT that hears a real person, and the gate problems that make a mic look open while nothing arrives."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [voice, tts, stt, whisper, kokoro, vad, audio, pipewire]
+---
+
+# A local voice pipeline
+
+Two independent halves that fail differently. Speech **out** fails loudly (nothing plays). Speech **in**
+fails silently (the mic is open, the recogniser returns empty strings, and everything reports healthy).
+
+## Speech out
+
+**The sink, not the file, is what people notice.** Generating a WAV that nobody hears is a zero.
+
+```bash
+# generate
+kokoro -m af_heart -o /tmp/say.wav "the line to speak"
+# play it, and be able to STOP it
+paplay /tmp/say.wav &
+```
+
+- **Name the voice explicitly.** A default that changes between releases is worse than a voice you do
+  not love. `af_heart` is the reference for a warm mid-range read.
+- **Play through the default sink**, and resolve that sink at play time — a Bluetooth headset that
+  connects mid-session moves it, and a hard-coded device name is how audio goes to a headset nobody is
+  wearing.
+- **Speech must be interruptible.** Barge-in — the moment the user starts talking, playback stops — is
+  the single feature that makes voice usable rather than a monologue. Implement it as: cancel the
+  player process on the first recognised speech frame.
+- **Keep utterances short and sentences complete.** Long generations sound like reading; 15–25 s of
+  speech at natural cadence is about one paragraph.
+- **Do not narrate the work.** Spoken output is for the answer, a real question, or a decision that is
+  needed. Never for tool calls, plans, or progress. Silence while working is correct.
+
+## Speech in
+
+### The failure that looks like a working mic
+
+A capture graph can look completely healthy — track live, no errors, no dropouts — and deliver **zero
+audio**, because the render side is suspended. On Linux/PipeWire, an idle Bluetooth or HDMI sink can
+suspend the render quantum while the capture track stays "live". The tell: frames arrive, energy is exactly
+zero forever, and nothing in the web-audio layer complains.
+
+Two defences:
+
+1. **Hold a silent render** on the current output device for the whole capture session, so the graph keeps
+   running.
+2. **Read the energy yourself** and treat a flat zero as a capture failure, not as silence from the room.
+
+### Energy gating — fixed gates, never adaptive
+
+A simple RMS gate decides when a clip starts and stops. Use **fixed thresholds**, measured on the actual
+hardware:
+
+```
+open  at RMS ≈ 450      # above this, speech has started
+close at RMS ≈ 280      # below this, keep listening a beat, then end the clip
+```
+
+**An adaptive noise floor will silently go deaf.** It converges onto whatever the loudest steady sound
+is, drifts above the voice, and then reports a healthy signal-to-noise ratio while gating out every word.
+It reads as "the microphone stopped working" and it is a maths problem, not hardware.
+
+Two more gates worth having:
+
+- **A minimum clip length** (drop < ~250 ms) and a **maximum** (force-flush at ~15 s) so a stuck gate
+  cannot hold a clip forever.
+- **A short pre-roll** (200–300 ms kept before the open threshold) so the first consonant is not clipped.
+  Word-initial fricatives are what a bad pre-roll eats, and it makes the first word of every sentence
+  unreliable.
+
+### Recognition
+
+- **Whisper-class models, locally.** `faster-whisper` with `small.en` or `medium.en` transcribes a 5 s clip
+  in roughly 2.5–3 s on a modern CPU. That is fast enough to feel live when clips are processed
+  concurrently.
+- **Do not put STT on the GPU** when the same machine is serving a model. Contention makes both slower,
+  and CPU inference on a small Whisper is already real-time enough.
+- **Vosk with a grammar built from a small word list is actively harmful.** Restricted grammars bias
+  decoding toward the listed words, so every out-of-vocabulary word becomes the nearest listed one —
+  confident, wrong, and unfixable downstream. A small Vosk model also tends to prepend a filler word
+  ("the") to everything. Prefer Whisper unless there is a specific reason.
+- **16 kHz, 16-bit, mono** is correct. Upsampling to 44.1 kHz adds bytes and no accuracy.
+- **Run transcription off the audio thread.** Ingest frames continuously; hand finished clips to a worker.
+  Blocking the capture loop on recognition drops the next clip.
+
+### The chunk protocol that keeps turns in order
+
+If capture, transcription and the chat turn are separate workers, they will reorder under load. A minimal
+protocol that holds up:
+
+```
+/chunk     push a finished audio clip
+/claim     take ownership of the next complete utterance
+/own       keep ownership while composing
+/release   hand back
+/state     what is pending, who owns what
+```
+
+The rule that matters: **a recognised utterance is not a turn until it is claimed**, and the owner must
+release it even on failure — otherwise one dropped turn locks the microphone forever.
+
+## Configuration traps
+
+- **A provider that points at nothing looks configured.** Setting `tts.provider = kokoro` without a
+  command or endpoint resolves to silence with no error. Verify the whole chain by speaking one line and
+  hearing it.
+- **Check which interpreter has the dependency.** `faster-whisper` installed in the system Python but not
+  the app's virtualenv means transcription fails inside the app while working in your shell.
+- **A local engine hot-reloads are not free.** A plugin or module reload drops state but not timers — an
+  orphaned capture handle keeps feeding the recogniser and every word comes back doubled. Stop the old
+  handle explicitly before starting a new one.
+- **Defaults matter more than features.** Ship push-to-talk; make continuous listening opt-in.
+
+## Verifying it end to end
+
+1. Speak one sentence; watch RMS rise above the open threshold and fall below the close threshold.
+2. Confirm the clip length is sane (not 40 ms, not 15 s).
+3. Confirm the transcript contains the words you said — print the raw text before any normalisation.
+4. Confirm the reply is spoken **and** that speaking over it stops it.
+5. Repeat with the output device changed (headphones → speakers) — that is where routing bugs live.
