+---
+name: web-research
+description: "Find a fact on the web without burning an hour: curl first, the source's own endpoint second, a real browser only when the page actually needs one."
+version: 1.0.0
+tags: [search, curl, fetches, cloudflare, paywall, wayback, scraping]
+---
+
+# Web research, cheapest rung first
+
+Most "the site blocked me" failures are a transport mistake, not a bot-detection problem. Most "the
+search engine found nothing" failures are a helper that threw away the HTTP status. This skill is the
+order of attack and the traps that make an empty result look like an answer.
+
+## The order of attack
+
+1. **`curl` the exact URL.** Sub-second. Correct for APIs, JSON, feeds, plain HTML.
+2. **The source's own API or summary endpoint** — almost every site that walls its HTML serves JSON on
+   the same host. `<host>/api/v1/...`, `/graphql`, `.json`, `/feed`, `/rss`, `/sitemap.xml`.
+3. **An archive** when the live page is gone or walled (Wayback first, `archive.today` second).
+4. **A real browser in debug mode** (`--remote-debugging-port`), your own profile, read
+   `document.body.innerText`. This is the rung that passes Cloudflare, because it *is* a browser.
+5. **Never**: re-running a failed engine. A method that failed twice will fail a third time.
+
+A browser page load costs seconds per page; a curl costs under one. Do not open a browser for a page
+curl can fetch, and do not narrate each rung while the user waits.
+
+## The false negative that costs the most
+
+**A fetch helper that does not capture the HTTP status reports a wall as "0 results".**
+
+```
+curl -s "$URL"                     # returns the body whatever the status
+```
+
+A `429` or a bot-challenge page parses to zero result links and prints as *nothing found* — which
+blames the query instead of the throttle, and sends you off rephrasing. Always:
+
+```
+code=$(curl -sS -L --compressed -A "$UA" -o /tmp/page.html -w '%{http_code}' "$URL")
+case "$code" in
+  200) ... ;;
+  429) echo "429 RATE LIMITED — change transport, not query" ;;
+  000) echo "connection failure — not a missing document" ;;
+  *)   echo "HTTP $code" ;;
+esac
+```
+
+Two empty outcomes are different diagnoses:
+
+- **200 with an empty body** → wrong identifier or genuinely absent. Re-check the ID before you change
+  transport.
+- **`000`, zero bytes, or a timeout** → transport. Change the user agent, the network path, or the rung.
+
+## Endpoint table — try these before searching
+
+| You need | Go here |
+|---|---|
+| A subject summary | Wikipedia REST: `/api/rest_v1/page/summary/<Article>` |
+| One field out of a Wikipedia infobox or table | `/w/index.php?title=<Article>&action=raw`, then grep. The summary endpoint is a stub on thin articles, and `innerText` collapses tables to a caption |
+| A US statute | `law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/<title>/<section>` |
+| A US case, full text | `law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/<volume>/<page>` |
+| A federal rule or notice | `federalregister.gov/api/v1/documents.json?conditions[term]=...` |
+| An economic series | `fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/fredgraph.csv?id=<SERIES>` |
+| A repo's file list without cloning | `api.github.com/repos/<o>/<r>/git/trees/main?recursive=1`, then `raw.githubusercontent.com/...` |
+| Model repo sizes, shards, quants | `huggingface.co/api/models/<repo>/tree/main?recursive=true` |
+| Everything one lab released, with dates | `huggingface.co/api/models?author=<org>&sort=lastModified&direction=-1&limit=30` |
+| Is this packaged for Arch | `aur.archlinux.org/rpc/v5/info?arg[]=<pkg>` — an empty `resultcount` is a real answer |
+| Does a Flatpak exist | `flathub.org/api/v2/appstream/<app-id>` |
+| Does a game exist / has it shipped | `store.steampowered.com/api/appdetails?appids=<id>&cc=us&l=en` → `release_date` |
+| A vendor datasheet (pins, voltages) | the vendor's own `resource.…/download/…pdf` path, not the marketing page |
+| A bill's roll-call vote | `clerk.house.gov/evs/<year>/roll<NNN>.xml` |
+
+**A CDN bot-wall on HTML is not a wall on the API.** Government and news sites regularly answer
+`curl` with *Access Denied* while a JSON API on the same domain serves fine. Look for `/api/` before
+concluding a number is unavailable.
+
+**A docs site whose body is JavaScript returns framework code, not documentation.** Look for the
+plain-text alternate the platform advertises (`llms.txt`, a `?plain=1` sibling, a `.md` path, a
+`<link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown">`); if there genuinely is none, treat it as
+needs-a-browser and use the debug-mode browser rather than dumping 200 KB of bundle into context.
+
+## When the search engine itself is walled
+
+Engines wall by **volume across a session**, not only per minute — a long research session ends with
+the first query of the next batch refused. Once that is set, spacing queries out buys nothing; change
+transport.
+
+- Alternate engine, once.
+- The same engine through a local FlareSolverr, if one is running:
+  `curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8191/v1 -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"cmd":"request.get","url":"<search URL>","maxTimeout":60000}'`
+  then read `solution.response`, strip `<script>`/`<style>` **before** tag-stripping, and grep the body
+  for a string you know belongs on the real page — `status: ok` means the challenge passed, not that
+  you got content.
+- A real browser over CDP, fresh profile, offscreen. That is the rung that works when everything
+  unauthenticated fails.
+- The **primary source instead of a search engine**. For a rate, a date, a filing, a release — the
+  announcing party's own page answers curl when every front-end will not, and it is the better source
+  anyway.
+
+Report the wall in one line and move on: `curl HTTP403 → trying the archive`. That line is the
+scoreboard; without it, a loop looks like work.
+
+## Archives and provenance
+
+```
+curl -sL "https://archive.org/wayback/available?url=<URL>"        # closest snapshot + timestamp
+curl -sL "https://web.archive.org/cdx/search/cdx?url=<URL>&output=json&limit=10"
+for d in archive.ph archive.md archive.li archive.is; do … done    # archive.today rotates domains
+```
+
+A snapshot is **context, not an answer**, whenever the question is about the present. Cite it with its
+date: "as archived 2026-08-06". Never present a snapshot as the live page.
+
+**Routes that lie** — HTTP 200 with a body that is not the page:
+
+- Google Cache (dead since mid-2024) — returns a search interstitial.
+- AMP caches — a ~300-byte meta-refresh stub pointing back at the blocked URL.
+- Rate-limit bodies — several KB of HTML that passes a size check. Grep for a string you know.
+- Search clients returning **zero** hits for every query at once — that is a challenge, not an absence.
+  A real search returns a variable number of hits.
+
+## Reading the result
+
+- **Extract in the same command that fetches.** A SERP is one large document with every result in
+  inline JSON; piping it through shell filters truncates at the capture window and buries the answer.
+  Write a small Python file (never a quoted one-liner — nested quotes die and the failure reads as
+  "no matches") and parse there.
+- **Never let a fetched page reach your context whole.** You wanted three lines.
+- **A search-hit count is not a fact.** Confirm on the source page before it goes in an answer.
+- **Deliver the content in the reply, not a path to it.** If someone asks you to show text, print the
+  text. Writing it to a file and answering with the filename is a detour that makes them go fetch it.
+- **Answer in the register asked.** A question that wants one line gets one line. A riff about a
+  vendor is not a research request — the correct number of searches there is zero.
+
+## When the batch is dead
+
+Three named methods on one URL and you stop. Write what is established to disk, separate it from what
+is merely believed, name the specific record that would settle the open question, and say so. A fifth
+pass on a walled engine is not thoroughness.
